IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `lsl-dates`
IMPORT `lsl-core`
IMPORT `lsl-seat`

-- Long Service Leave Act 1955 (NSW) -- cases for the seat of the worker (lsl-seat.l4). Every expected value was worked
-- by hand from the deposited text (the section is named on each group), never copied from the encoding's output; day
-- counts and fractions are shown where they matter. Edges: 5, 10 and 15 completed years and the day before, the
-- day-not-counted that delays the 10th anniversary, a 29 February start, the 2-month and 12-month return windows to
-- the day and a day over (with a month-end), the COVID-19 period's first and last days and the days outside, the
-- one-month notice to the day and a day late, the 6-year recovery window to the day and a day over (with 29 February),
-- the s 3(2C) threshold met exactly and exceeded by a dollar, and the (5AA) and final-pay caps on a deduction.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Cases: the worker`

`ANY` MEANS `the date` 1 1 2000

`EMP` MEANS `I am employed by the employer, on salary, wages, piecework or commission, or as an outworker or under a labour-only contract`
`BAILEE` MEANS `I ply for hire, deliver goods or carry passengers in a vehicle or vessel bailed to me by the other person, in work for which an award or industrial agreement fixes a rate`
`IRDEEMED` MEANS `I am deemed an employee of the other person by the Industrial Relations Act 1996`
`OWN` MEANS `I am not employed: I work on my own account`
`NOEX` MEANS `my employer has no exemption from the Industrial Relations Commission`
`EXELECTED` MEANS `my employer has an exemption under s 5(2) and I elected in writing, in time, to come under this Act`
`EXNOT` MEANS `my employer has an exemption under s 5(2) and I did not elect to come under this Act`

`COVYES` MEANS `yes: you are a worker, and s 4 entitles you to long service leave on ordinary pay for your service with the employer (s 3(1), s 4(1))`
`COVNOTEMP` MEANS `no: a worker means a person employed (s 3(1)), and you are not employed, so the Act gives you no long service leave`
`COVBAIL` MEANS `no: the tribunal was satisfied that the bailment was bona fide and not made to avoid the Act, so you are not deemed a worker (s 3(3))`
`COVMET` MEANS `no: a person who is a worker under the Long Service Leave (Metalliferous Mining Industry) Act 1963 is not a worker under this Act (s 3(1))`
`COVAWARD` MEANS `no: s 4 does not apply to you because your award or agreement gives long service leave more favourable than s 4; you take it under the award or agreement (s 5(1)(a))`
`COVACT` MEANS `no: s 4 does not apply to you because another Act provides long service leave for your class of workers (s 5(1)(b))`
`COVEXEMPT` MEANS `no: your employer is exempt from the Act's long service leave provisions for you, and its scheme applies instead (s 5(2)(a), (c))`

§§ `s 3, s 5: coverage`

#ASSERT `Does the Act give me long service leave` `EMP` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `NOEX` EQUALS `COVYES`
#ASSERT `Does the Act give me long service leave` `OWN` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `NOEX` EQUALS `COVNOTEMP`
#ASSERT `Does the Act give me long service leave` `BAILEE` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE `NOEX` EQUALS `COVBAIL`
#ASSERT `Does the Act give me long service leave` `BAILEE` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `NOEX` EQUALS `COVYES`
#ASSERT `Does the Act give me long service leave` `EMP` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE `NOEX` EQUALS `COVYES`
#ASSERT `Does the Act give me long service leave` `IRDEEMED` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `NOEX` EQUALS `COVYES`
#ASSERT `Does the Act give me long service leave` `EMP` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE `NOEX` EQUALS `COVMET`
#ASSERT `Does the Act give me long service leave` `EMP` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE `NOEX` EQUALS `COVAWARD`
#ASSERT `Does the Act give me long service leave` `EMP` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE `NOEX` EQUALS `COVACT`
#ASSERT `Does the Act give me long service leave` `EMP` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `EXNOT` EQUALS `COVEXEMPT`
#ASSERT `Does the Act give me long service leave` `EMP` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `EXELECTED` EQUALS `COVYES`

§§ `s 5(2)(c)(i): the last day to elect out of an exempt scheme (3 months after the later day)`

-- Employed before the exemption: 15 January 2026 + 3 months = 15 April 2026.
#ASSERT `By when must I elect to come under the Act` (`the date` 15 1 2026) (`the date` 1 3 2020) EQUALS `the date` 15 4 2026
-- Employed after the exemption: 30 November 2025 + 3 months = 28 February 2026 (February 2026 has 28 days).
#ASSERT `By when must I elect to come under the Act` (`the date` 1 1 2020) (`the date` 30 11 2025) EQUALS `the date` 28 2 2026
-- 31 August 2023 + 3 months = 30 November 2023.
#ASSERT `By when must I elect to come under the Act` (`the date` 31 8 2023) (`the date` 31 8 2023) EQUALS `the date` 30 11 2023

§§ `s 4(11), (13), s 15C: breaks`

`TERMS` MEANS `an absence under the terms of my employment, such as paid leave`
`ILL` MEANS `an absence because of my illness or injury`
`AVOID` MEANS `my employer ended or interrupted my service to avoid long service leave or sick leave obligations`
`DISPUTE` MEANS `an industrial dispute`
`SLACK` MEANS `my employer ended or interrupted my service because of slackness of trade`
`LEAVE` MEANS `an absence for any cause with my employer's leave`
`OTHERREASON` MEANS `my employer ended or interrupted my service for another reason`
`RESIGNED` MEANS `I resigned, or left for a reason of my own, and later came back`
`COVID` MEANS `I was stood down without pay because of the COVID-19 pandemic`
`TRANSMIT` MEANS `the business I worked in was transferred to a new employer, and I went with it`
`RELATED` MEANS `I moved to a corporation related to my employer (its holding company, its subsidiary, or another subsidiary of its holding company)`
`APPRENTICE` MEANS `I had finished an apprenticeship with this employer, or with a corporation related to it`

`CCOUNTS` MEANS `yes, continuous, and the time counts as service: the break was an absence under the terms of your employment or because of illness or injury (s 4(11)(a1)(i), (ii))`
`CNOTCOUNTED` MEANS `yes, continuous, but the days of the break are not counted as service (s 4(11)(a1)(iii)-(vi))`
`CBACK2` MEANS `yes, continuous, because you came back within 2 months; the days of the break are not counted as service (s 4(11)(a1)(vii))`
`CLATE` MEANS `no: you came back more than 2 months after your employer ended your service, so service before the break is not continuous with service after it (s 4(11)(a), (a1)(vii))`
`COWN` MEANS `no: service means continuous service (s 4(11)(a)), and leaving of your own accord is not one of the breaks the Act saves, so service before the break does not count`
`CCOVID` MEANS `yes, continuous, and the time counts: you were stood down without pay because of COVID-19 during the prescribed period, and you kept accruing long service leave (s 15C(1))`
`CCOVIDOUT` MEANS `s 15C does not reach this: the stand-down began outside the prescribed period (11 March 2020 to 30 September 2021, unless a regulation prescribed a later day, not after 31 March 2022); see whether a head of s 4(11)(a1) applies`
`CTRANSMIT` MEANS `yes, continuous, and your service with the old employer counts as service with the new one (s 4(11)(c))`
`CRELATED` MEANS `yes, continuous, and your service with the first corporation counts as service with the related one, because the new contract was made within 2 months after the first one ended; the days between are not counted (s 4(13)(b)(ii), (c))`
`CRELATEDLATE` MEANS `no: a move to a related corporation keeps your service only if the new contract was made within 2 months after the first one ended (s 4(13)(b)(ii))`
`CAPPR` MEANS `yes, your apprenticeship counts as service, because you entered the contract of employment within 12 months after completing it; the days between are not counted (s 4(11)(b), s 4(13)(d))`
`CAPPRLATE` MEANS `no: the apprenticeship counts only if you entered the contract of employment within 12 months after completing it (s 4(11)(b), s 4(13)(d))`

#ASSERT `Was my service continuous despite this break` `TERMS` (`the date` 1 3 2024) (`the date` 1 4 2024) EQUALS `CCOUNTS`
#ASSERT `Was my service continuous despite this break` `ILL` (`the date` 1 3 2024) (`the date` 1 9 2024) EQUALS `CCOUNTS`
-- 1 to 15 March 2024: 14 days.
#ASSERT `Was my service continuous despite this break` `AVOID` (`the date` 1 3 2024) (`the date` 15 3 2024) EQUALS `CNOTCOUNTED` 14
-- 1 January to 1 February 2025: 31 days.
#ASSERT `Was my service continuous despite this break` `DISPUTE` (`the date` 1 1 2025) (`the date` 1 2 2025) EQUALS `CNOTCOUNTED` 31
-- 10 June to 10 July 2024: 30 days.
#ASSERT `Was my service continuous despite this break` `SLACK` (`the date` 10 6 2024) (`the date` 10 7 2024) EQUALS `CNOTCOUNTED` 30
-- 1 January 2024 to 1 January 2025: 366 days (2024 is a leap year).
#ASSERT `Was my service continuous despite this break` `LEAVE` (`the date` 1 1 2024) (`the date` 1 1 2025) EQUALS `CNOTCOUNTED` 366
-- s 4(11)(a1)(vii): 15 January 2025 + 2 months = 15 March 2025; 17 + 28 + 14 = 59 days.
#ASSERT `Was my service continuous despite this break` `OTHERREASON` (`the date` 15 1 2025) (`the date` 15 3 2025) EQUALS `CBACK2` 59
#ASSERT `Was my service continuous despite this break` `OTHERREASON` (`the date` 15 1 2025) (`the date` 16 3 2025) EQUALS `CLATE`
-- 31 December 2024 + 2 months = 28 February 2025; 31 + 28 = 59 days. 1 March 2025 is too late.
#ASSERT `Was my service continuous despite this break` `OTHERREASON` (`the date` 31 12 2024) (`the date` 28 2 2025) EQUALS `CBACK2` 59
#ASSERT `Was my service continuous despite this break` `OTHERREASON` (`the date` 31 12 2024) (`the date` 1 3 2025) EQUALS `CLATE`
#ASSERT `Was my service continuous despite this break` `RESIGNED` (`the date` 1 3 2024) (`the date` 8 3 2024) EQUALS `COWN`
-- s 15C(3): 11 March 2020 to 30 September 2021.
#ASSERT `Was my service continuous despite this break` `COVID` (`the date` 11 3 2020) (`the date` 1 7 2020) EQUALS `CCOVID`
#ASSERT `Was my service continuous despite this break` `COVID` (`the date` 30 9 2021) (`the date` 1 11 2021) EQUALS `CCOVID`
#ASSERT `Was my service continuous despite this break` `COVID` (`the date` 10 3 2020) (`the date` 1 7 2020) EQUALS `CCOVIDOUT`
#ASSERT `Was my service continuous despite this break` `COVID` (`the date` 1 10 2021) (`the date` 1 11 2021) EQUALS `CCOVIDOUT`
#ASSERT `Was my service continuous despite this break` `TRANSMIT` (`the date` 1 7 2023) (`the date` 1 7 2023) EQUALS `CTRANSMIT`
-- s 4(13)(b)(ii): 1 May 2025 + 2 months = 1 July 2025; 31 + 30 = 61 days.
#ASSERT `Was my service continuous despite this break` `RELATED` (`the date` 1 5 2025) (`the date` 1 7 2025) EQUALS `CRELATED` 61
#ASSERT `Was my service continuous despite this break` `RELATED` (`the date` 1 5 2025) (`the date` 2 7 2025) EQUALS `CRELATEDLATE`
-- s 4(11)(b): 1 July 2024 + 12 months = 1 July 2025; 365 days.
#ASSERT `Was my service continuous despite this break` `APPRENTICE` (`the date` 1 7 2024) (`the date` 1 7 2025) EQUALS `CAPPR` 365
#ASSERT `Was my service continuous despite this break` `APPRENTICE` (`the date` 1 7 2024) (`the date` 2 7 2025) EQUALS `CAPPRLATE`

