§§ `s 4(10), (10A): notice`

`N1` MEANS `yes: you were given at least one month's notice of the date your leave begins (s 4(10))`
`N2` MEANS `yes: you were given less than one month's notice, but you agreed to the shorter notice (s 4(10A))`

#ASSERT `Was I given enough notice of my leave` (`the date` 15 3 2026) (`the date` 15 2 2026) FALSE EQUALS `N1`
#ASSERT `Was I given enough notice of my leave` (`the date` 15 3 2026) (`the date` 16 2 2026) FALSE EQUALS `no: your employer had to give you at least one month's notice of the date your leave begins, that is by the day shown, unless you agreed to less (s 4(10), (10A))` (`the date` 15 2 2026)
#ASSERT `Was I given enough notice of my leave` (`the date` 15 3 2026) (`the date` 16 2 2026) TRUE EQUALS `N2`
-- 31 March 2026 less one month = 28 February 2026.
#ASSERT `Was I given enough notice of my leave` (`the date` 31 3 2026) (`the date` 28 2 2026) FALSE EQUALS `N1`
#ASSERT `Was I given enough notice of my leave` (`the date` 31 3 2026) (`the date` 1 3 2026) FALSE EQUALS `no: your employer had to give you at least one month's notice of the date your leave begins, that is by the day shown, unless you agreed to less (s 4(10), (10A))` (`the date` 28 2 2026)
-- 29 March 2024 less one month = 29 February 2024 (a leap year).
#ASSERT `Was I given enough notice of my leave` (`the date` 29 3 2024) (`the date` 1 3 2024) FALSE EQUALS `no: your employer had to give you at least one month's notice of the date your leave begins, that is by the day shown, unless you agreed to less (s 4(10), (10A))` (`the date` 29 2 2024)

§§ `s 4(5), (5A), (5B), (8), (13)(e): being paid instead of taking leave`

`P1` MEANS `no: while you are employed, your employer must not pay you instead of giving you the leave, and you must not accept such a payment; no agreement can change that (s 4(8), s 7)`
`P2` MEANS `no: a move to a related corporation is not a termination (s 4(13)(e)); your service carries over and the leave is still to be taken, not paid out (s 4(8))`
`P3` MEANS `yes: you are taken to have begun the leave on the day your employment ended, and your employer must forthwith pay you in full your ordinary pay for the untaken leave, less anything already paid for it (s 4(5)(a))`
`P4` MEANS `yes, to your personal representative: on the personal representative's request the employer must pay in full the ordinary pay for the untaken leave, less anything already paid for it (s 4(5)(b)); the personal representative may recover it as you could (s 4(9))`
`P5` MEANS `not yet: you are registered under a portable long service scheme for building or contract cleaning workers, so your employer must not give you leave or pay you for it unless you apply (s 4(5A), (5B))`

#ASSERT `Can I be paid for my leave instead of taking it` `STILL` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `P1`
#ASSERT `Can I be paid for my leave instead of taking it` `STILL` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `P1`
#ASSERT `Can I be paid for my leave instead of taking it` `MOVED` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `P2`
#ASSERT `Can I be paid for my leave instead of taking it` `DISMISSED` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `P3`
#ASSERT `Can I be paid for my leave instead of taking it` `MISCONDUCT` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `P3`
#ASSERT `Can I be paid for my leave instead of taking it` `QUIT` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `P3`
#ASSERT `Can I be paid for my leave instead of taking it` `DISMISSED` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `P5`
#ASSERT `Can I be paid for my leave instead of taking it` `DISMISSED` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `P3`
#ASSERT `Can I be paid for my leave instead of taking it` `DIED` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `P4`

§§ `s 4(5)(a), (b): the payment on termination`

-- 10 years, dismissed: 10 x 3/15 = 2 months = 26/3 weeks x 1500 = 13000; less 1000 already paid = 12000.
#ASSERT `How much must I be paid for my untaken leave` `START` (`the date` 30 6 2020) 0 `DISMISSED` 0 1500 0 EQUALS 13000
#ASSERT `How much must I be paid for my untaken leave` `START` (`the date` 30 6 2020) 0 `DISMISSED` 0 1500 1000 EQUALS 12000
#ASSERT `How much must I be paid for my untaken leave` `START` (`the date` 30 6 2020) 0 `STILL` 0 1500 0 EQUALS 0
-- 12 years, resigning, 26/3 weeks taken: 26/15 weeks x 1500 = 2600.
#ASSERT `How much must I be paid for my untaken leave` `START` (`the date` 30 6 2022) 0 `QUIT` (26 DIVIDED BY 3) 1500 0 EQUALS 2600
-- 52/5 weeks x 1000 = 10400, but 20000 already paid: 0.
#ASSERT `How much must I be paid for my untaken leave` `START` (`the date` 30 6 2022) 0 `QUIT` 0 1000 20000 EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `How much must I be paid for my untaken leave` `START` (`the date` 30 6 2018) 0 `QUIT` 0 1500 0 EQUALS 0
-- 7 years, death: 91/15 weeks x 1200 = 7280, to the personal representative.
#ASSERT `How much must I be paid for my untaken leave` `START` (`the date` 30 6 2017) 0 `DIED` 0 1200 0 EQUALS 7280

§§ `s 4(5)(c), (5AA): deduction for leave taken in advance`

-- 3 years, dismissed (no entitlement), 2 weeks taken in advance at 1000: min(2000, 2 x 1100, 5000) = 2000.
#ASSERT `How much can be deducted for leave I took in advance` `START` (`the date` 30 6 2013) 0 `DISMISSED` 2 TRUE 1000 1100 5000 EQUALS 2000
-- (5AA): ordinary pay at the end only 900: 2 x 900 = 1800.
#ASSERT `How much can be deducted for leave I took in advance` `START` (`the date` 30 6 2013) 0 `DISMISSED` 2 TRUE 1000 900 5000 EQUALS 1800
-- Only from remuneration payable on termination: 1500.
#ASSERT `How much can be deducted for leave I took in advance` `START` (`the date` 30 6 2013) 0 `DISMISSED` 2 TRUE 1000 1100 1500 EQUALS 1500
#ASSERT `How much can be deducted for leave I took in advance` `START` (`the date` 30 6 2013) 0 `DISMISSED` 2 FALSE 1000 1100 5000 EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `How much can be deducted for leave I took in advance` `START` (`the date` 30 6 2013) 0 `STILL` 2 TRUE 1000 1100 5000 EQUALS 0
-- 6 years, dismissed: entitlement 1.2 months = 26/5 weeks; 8 weeks taken: excess 14/5 weeks x 1000 = 2800.
#ASSERT `How much can be deducted for leave I took in advance` `START` (`the date` 30 6 2016) 0 `DISMISSED` 8 TRUE 1000 1000 10000 EQUALS 2800
#ASSERT `How much can be deducted for leave I took in advance` `START` (`the date` 30 6 2016) 0 `DISMISSED` 5 TRUE 1000 1000 10000 EQUALS 0

§§ `s 12: recovering unpaid pay (6 years)`

#ASSERT `By when must I apply to recover my unpaid long service leave pay` (`the date` 10 3 2020) EQUALS `the date` 10 3 2026
#ASSERT `By when must I apply to recover my unpaid long service leave pay` (`the date` 29 2 2020) EQUALS `the date` 28 2 2026

`RIN` MEANS `in time: the payment fell due within the 6 years before your application (s 12(1))`
`RLATE` MEANS `too late: the payment fell due more than 6 years before your application, so s 12 does not let the court order it (s 12(1))`
`RSTILL` MEANS `still in time: you have not applied yet, and an application today would be within 6 years of the day the payment fell due (s 12(1))`
`RGONE` MEANS `too late: you have not applied, and more than 6 years have passed since the payment fell due (s 12(1))`

#ASSERT `Is my claim for unpaid long service leave pay in time` (`the date` 10 3 2020) TRUE (`the date` 10 3 2026) `ANY` EQUALS `RIN`
#ASSERT `Is my claim for unpaid long service leave pay in time` (`the date` 10 3 2020) TRUE (`the date` 11 3 2026) `ANY` EQUALS `RLATE`
#ASSERT `Is my claim for unpaid long service leave pay in time` (`the date` 10 3 2020) FALSE `ANY` (`the date` 10 3 2026) EQUALS `RSTILL`
#ASSERT `Is my claim for unpaid long service leave pay in time` (`the date` 10 3 2020) FALSE `ANY` (`the date` 11 3 2026) EQUALS `RGONE`
#ASSERT `Is my claim for unpaid long service leave pay in time` (`the date` 29 2 2020) TRUE (`the date` 28 2 2026) `ANY` EQUALS `RIN`
#ASSERT `Is my claim for unpaid long service leave pay in time` (`the date` 29 2 2020) TRUE (`the date` 1 3 2026) `ANY` EQUALS `RLATE`
#ASSERT `Is my claim for unpaid long service leave pay in time` (`the date` 10 3 2020) TRUE (`the date` 10 3 2020) `ANY` EQUALS `RIN`

§§ `s 8(3): records; s 10, s 10A, s 11: penalties`

#ASSERT `Until when must my employer keep my record` (`the date` 30 6 2026) EQUALS `the date` 30 6 2032
#ASSERT `Until when must my employer keep my record` (`the date` 29 2 2024) EQUALS `the date` 28 2 2030

#ASSERT `What penalty does my employer face` FALSE EQUALS `your employer is liable to a penalty of up to 20 penalty units for each contravention, such as not giving or not paying your long service leave or not keeping your record (s 10(1)); you, as a person whose rights are impaired, an inspector or a union secretary may take the proceedings in the Local Court, which may also order the payment due to you, and that order bars a later s 12 claim for it (s 11(1), (2))`
#ASSERT `What penalty does my employer face` TRUE EQUALS `your employer is liable to a penalty of up to 20 penalty units for each contravention, such as not giving or not paying your long service leave or not keeping your record (s 10(1)); you, as a person whose rights are impaired, an inspector or a union secretary may take the proceedings in the Local Court, which may also order the payment due to you, and that order bars a later s 12 claim for it (s 11(1), (2)); because your employer is a corporation, a director or manager who knew or ought reasonably to have known of an offence under s 4 or s 8 and did not take all reasonable steps to prevent it is liable to the same maximum (s 10A)`
